{"answer":"Logistic regression is a statistical model used for binary (or multi‑class) classification. It models the probability that a given input belongs to a particular class as a logistic (sigmoid) function of a linear combination of the input features. In its simplest form, for a binary outcome \(y \in \{0,1\}\) and feature vector \(\mathbf{x}\), the model estimates parameters \( oldsymbol{ eta}\) such that:

\[
P(y=1 \mid \mathbf{x}) = \sigma( oldsymbol{ eta}^	op \mathbf{x}) =  rac{1}{1 + e^{- oldsymbol{ eta}^	op \mathbf{x}}}
\]

The coefficients \( oldsymbol{ eta}\) are typically estimated by maximizing the likelihood of the observed data (maximum likelihood estimation). Logistic regression can be extended to handle multiple classes (multinomial logistic regression) or ordered categories (ordinal logistic regression). It is widely used because it provides probabilistic outputs, is relatively simple to implement, and works well when the relationship between the log‑odds of the outcome and the predictors is approximately linear.
"}

